between one and seven years and will be refinanced at lower rates when they mature. It notes the refinancing and liquidity risks this creates.","iso":"2026-11-24","paper_title":"Item 7.1 - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://cardiff.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s100108/100108.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Cardiff forecasts \u00a39.445m parking enforcement surplus, \u00a3763,000 above target for 2026-27","lead":"Cardiff Council's civil parking enforcement account is forecast to make a surplus of \u00a39.445m this year, \u00a3763,000 (9%) above its target of \u00a38.682m, according to the month 4 budget monitoring report (the in-year check of spending against budget) going to Cabinet on 15 October.\n\nFour sources account for the gain. Income from moving traffic offences is forecast \u00a3305,000 above target, which the report attributes to improved recovery rates on penalty charge notices. Income from the notices themselves is \u00a3290,000 higher, from increased enforcement activity. Off-street parking is \u00a3243,000 up, from improved performance at individual locations and more events in the city. Resident parking permits are \u00a340,000 ahead, even after a new rate was introduced.\n\nThe report says the account's performance provides capacity to offset overspends elsewhere in the council. The papers give no figures here for those overspends or for how much of the surplus would be used against them.","iso":"2026-10-15","paper_title":"2026-10-15 Month 4","paper_url":"https://cardiff.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s99392/99392.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Cardiff Council faces \u00a3167m budget gap over four years as savings plans fall short","lead":"Cardiff Council is facing a projected budget shortfall of \u00a3167.1 million between 2027/28 and 2030/31, the Governance and Audit Committee heard on 24 September. The council balanced its 2025/26 budget but acknowledged that delivery of its savings programme is proving uneven: efficiency savings hit 88 per cent of target at \u00a310.9 million, while service change proposals achieved only 76 per cent at \u00a32.3 million. The Audit Committee noted that while the overall position was balanced, some directorates recorded significant overspends requiring control improvements. The council is also facing sustained demand pressures in social care, schools and education, particularly in support for additional learning needs. In a separate governance change, the former Section 151 Officer (chief finance officer) was promoted to Chief Executive in August, with the Deputy Section 151 Officer holding the role on an interim basis for up to 12 months while the senior management structure is reviewed. The council has been explicit about the risk that a Chief Executive permanently holding both roles would compromise independent financial challenge \u2014 guidance from the profession's standards body requires the Section 151 Officer to operate independently from the Chief Executive.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Appointment of Section 151 Officer","paper_url":"https://cardiff.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s97282/97282.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-11-24","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","paper_title":"Item 9.1 - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://cardiff.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s100125/100125.pdf","headline":"Changes effective 1 April 2026, including removal of the age 75 cap on death grants, are expected to increase Fund liabilities.","detail":"The changes are the first phase of the Government's Access and Fairness proposals. They include equalisation of survivor benefits, with potential backdating, and removal of the requirement to pay unpaid death grants to personal representatives after two years.","category":"other","matters":"A liability increase that the Fund cannot yet measure affects how finance directors assess their own fund's exposure, and will show up in future valuations.","figures":"","quote":"While these changes are expected to increase Fund liabilities, the financial impact cannot yet be reliably measured.","read_on":"2026-11-17","material":true},{"iso":"2026-11-24","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","paper_title":"Item 9.1 - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://cardiff.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s100125/100125.pdf","headline":"The accounts name Ian Allwood as Interim Section 151 Officer for 2025/26, and in a separate note name Christopher Lee as Section 151 Officer in 2025/26.","detail":"Ian Allwood signs the Responsible Financial Officer certificate. Christopher Lee also served as a transitional member of the WPP IM Co board during the year.","category":"people","matters":"A change of section 151 officer is a governance event that finance directors at other councils track, and the paper's two descriptions of the role for the same year need reconciling.","figures":"","quote":"In 2025/26 that officer was Ian Allwood who holds the statutory post of Interim Section 151 Officer","read_on":"2026-11-17","material":true},{"iso":"2026-11-24","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","paper_title":"Item 9.1 - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://cardiff.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s100125/100125.pdf","headline":"Auditor General for Wales reports the 2025/26 Fund accounts give a true and fair view, with no material uncertainty on going concern.","detail":"The auditor reports nothing to report on the narrative report or on the matters reported by exception. The paper also notes that the Government Actuary's Department review of the 2025 valuations has not yet been completed.","category":"audit","matters":"Finance directors at peer councils and LGPS funds benchmark their own pension fund assurance against this opinion and the outstanding GAD review.","figures":"","quote":"give a true and fair view of the financial transactions of the pension fund during the year ended 31 March 2026","read_on":"2026-11-17","material":true},{"iso":"2026-11-24","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","paper_title":"Item 9.1 - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://cardiff.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s100125/100125.pdf","headline":"The Fund paid \u00a3158,825 into WPP IM Co as capital in 2025/26, while the company's FCA authorisation remained pending at the reporting date.","detail":"WPP IM Co is 1/8th owned by each of the eight Welsh LGPS funds. Each fund will be required to provide regulatory capital during 2026-27 and will be charged fees depending on services taken and assets under management.","category":"governance","matters":"Every Welsh fund must fund regulatory capital and pay fees to the new company, so the FCA timetable affects the cost base and governance of each fund in the pool.","figures":"\u00a3158,825 (Fund payment into WPP IM Co, 2025/26); 1/8th (Fund's ownership share); 8 (number of Welsh LGPS funds)","quote":"At the reporting date, authorisation remained pending and the WPP IM Co was not yet undertaking regulated investment management activities.","read_on":"2026-11-17","material":false},{"iso":"2026-11-24","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","paper_title":"Item 9.1 - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://cardiff.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s100125/100125.pdf","headline":"Cardiff University exited the Fund in 2025/26, with a surplus exit payment of \u00a315.705 million.","detail":"The payment is included in the employer figures for the year. The paper notes a comparable exit in 2024/25, when Eisteddfod Genedlaethol Cymru paid a surplus exit payment of \u00a32.450 million.","category":"income","matters":"Surplus exit payments add to the Fund's cash and assets, and finance directors at councils with admitted or scheduled bodies that may leave will want to know the size of such payments.","figures":"\u00a315.705 million (surplus exit payment, Cardiff University); \u00a32.450 million (2024/25 surplus exit payment, Eisteddfod Genedlaethol Cymru)